 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_nova_scotia",
   "title": "New Scotland",
   "text": "Nova Scotia is Latin for New Scotland. The name comes from a charter of 1621, when James VI of Scotland and I of England granted the land to Sir William Alexander. Scots in great numbers only came later, from 1773, when the ship Hector landed some 190 Highlanders at Pictou."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_clearances",
   "title": "The Clearances",
   "text": "From the late 1700s to the mid 1800s many Highland landlords cleared tenants off their land to make way for sheep. Whole townships were emptied, and tens of thousands of Gaels emigrated, many of them to Cape Breton, where the hills and the sea looked a little like home."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_gaelic",
   "title": "G\u00e0idhlig in Canada",
   "text": "At one time Scottish Gaelic was spoken by tens of thousands of people in Nova Scotia. It faded over the 20th century, but it is taught again in schools and community classes, and Nova Scotia has an Office of Gaelic Affairs."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_college",
   "title": "The Gaelic College",
   "text": "A few miles up the road at St Ann's is the Gaelic College, Colaisde na G\u00e0idhlig, founded in 1938. It teaches the language, piping, Highland dance and fiddle. At Iona, on the lake, the Highland Village, Baile nan G\u00e0idheal, shows how the first Gaelic settlers lived."
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_bell",
   "title": "Beinn Bhreagh",
   "text": "Alexander Graham Bell, born in Edinburgh in 1847, spent his summers here from 1885 at Beinn Bhreagh, Gaelic for beautiful mountain, across the bay. He died there in 1922 and is buried on the mountain. Visitors then and now say the hills and water look like the Highlands."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_silver_dart",
   "title": "The Silver Dart",
   "text": "On 23 February 1909 the Silver Dart, built by Bell's Aerial Experiment Association, took off from the ice of Baddeck Bay with J. A. D. McCurdy at the controls. It was the first powered, controlled flight in Canada. The ice held. Everyone was relieved."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_bras_dor",
   "title": "The Bras d'Or Lake",
   "text": "Not quite a lake: the Bras d'Or is an inland sea of salt and fresh water, nearly cutting Cape Breton in two. It is a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve. Bald eagles nest round its shores, and Bell tested his hydrofoil boats on it; the HD-4 set a world water speed record here in 1919."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_baddeck_celtic_colours",
   "title": "Celtic Colours",
   "text": "Every October since 1997, the Celtic Colours festival fills halls all round Cape Breton with fiddlers, pipers, singers and step dancers, just as the maples turn red and gold. Cape Breton fiddling keeps an older Scottish style alive, played for dancers."
  }
 ],
